        "problem": "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}}{6x+3}.$",
        "worked_solution": "Let's simplify the expression by factoring both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}}{6x+3} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}(2x+1)}{3(2x+1)}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the denominator is never zero, we conclude that this function has no vertical asymptotes. A plot of the function is below. Note that it is not defined at $x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/assets/7ca0a626369d11d7d3cd8e2d99cbfb79eb37c534b86a1df3f43aaeff5fb932e3.png)",
